@* RangeSelector's Overview, written by hand: it's a .cs component, so its usage, accessibility and
   keys are here rather than in a doc comment. In a chart, on its own to filter by, spans of your
   own, labels. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A range selector is a strip that draws a whole range small, such as a year of days, with a window over the part chosen. It suits long runs of dates, where the reader looks closer at a month and needs to see where it sits in the year. To pick a range of plain numbers, such as prices, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/range-slider">RangeSlider</Anchor>; for two dates typed or picked from a calendar, a <Anchor Href="docs/components/date-range-field">DateRangeField</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">A strip under a chart</Heading>
<TextBlock>In an upright <Anchor Href="docs/components/chart">Chart</Anchor>, put <Code>&lt;RangeSelector /&gt;</Code> after the series. The strip sits under the plot and draws the chart's series small, and the window is the part of the chart in view. Dragging the window pans the chart, and dragging a handle widens or narrows it.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="range-selector-strip" />
<TextBlock>Clicking the strip outside the window moves the window there. Each handle's flag says where it is, and the two merge into one when the window is narrow. When the chart's own axis already shows what's in view, <Code>HideFlags</Code> leaves the flags out; screen readers still hear each handle's value.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Range buttons in a chart</Heading>
<TextBlock>To add buttons for the last 1, 3 and 6 months, the year to date, a year and everything, set <Code>Ranges</Code>. The pressed button is the span in view.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="range-selector-in-chart" />
<TextBlock>A button whose span would show everything is left out, so a chart of four months has no 6M or 1Y. A range selector turns on the chart's zoom, so the plot zooms by dragging, the wheel and the keys too. To open the chart on part of its arguments, or to learn in C# what's in view, set the chart's <Code>View</Code>; see <Anchor Href="docs/components/chart/zoom">Zoom</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>The strip moves with <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor>, with no round trip, on static pages too. With JavaScript off, it shows the part in view and nothing moves. A range selector needs an upright chart; in a chart with bars across, it throws.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Filtering by a date range</Heading>
<TextBlock>On its own, a range selector picks a range to use elsewhere, such as the dates a grid lists or a total sums. Give it the <Code>Items</Code> to draw, what each is at as <Code>Argument</Code>, a <Code>Label</Code>, and one or more series inside it, such as a <Code>ChartAreaSeries</Code>. Bind the range with <Code>@@bind-View</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="range-selector-filter" />
<TextBlock><Code>View</Code> is a <Code>ChartView</Code>: <Code>From</Code> and <Code>To</Code> are the first and last arguments chosen, both included, as the argument's own type (a <Code>DateOnly</Code> here). A null end is the data's own end, and a null view is everything. The view is reported as each drag or key settles, not while dragging, so it suits a query per change.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>Reporting the range needs an interactive page. On static pages the strip still moves, but C# doesn't learn the range.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Spans of your own</Heading>
<TextBlock>For spans the app names, such as fiscal quarters, set <Code>Presets</Code> to a list of <Code>DateRangePreset</Code>, each a label and its first and last days. They show as buttons beside the <Code>Ranges</Code> ones, or in their place. <Code>DateRangePreset.Common(today)</Code> gives the same spans as <Anchor Href="docs/components/date-range-field">DateRangeField</Anchor>'s.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Presets</Code>
<TextBlock>The strip is 4rem tall; <Code>Height</Code> sets another.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>Screen readers hear the strip's <Code>Label</Code> as the name of its group, and each handle by name with the argument it's at. The other words come from the app's text. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>IzzyText</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>RangeSelectorName</Code></td><td>The strip's name when it has no <Code>Label</Code></td><td>"Range in view"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>RangeSelectorStart</Code>, <Code>RangeSelectorEnd</Code></td><td>The handles' names</td><td>"Start", "End"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>RangeMonths</Code>, <Code>RangeYearToDate</Code>, <Code>RangeYear</Code>, <Code>RangeAll</Code></td><td>The range buttons' text; <Code>{0}</Code> is the number of months</td><td>"{0}M", "YTD", "1Y", "All"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>RangeMonthName</Code>, <Code>RangeMonthsName</Code>, <Code>RangeYearToDateName</Code>, <Code>RangeYearName</Code></td><td>The range buttons' names for screen readers</td><td>"Last month", "Last {0} months", "Year to date", "Last year"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

<Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
<Prose Size="ProseSize.Md">
    <ul>
        <li>The strip is a group named by <Code>Label</Code>. Its drawing and its dates are hidden from screen readers; in a chart, "Show data" lists every value.</li>
        <li>Each handle is a slider, the start first. Screen readers hear its name, "Start" or "End", and the argument it's at.</li>
        <li>The range buttons are buttons with full names, such as "Last 3 months", and the one in view is pressed.</li>
        <li>In a chart, screen readers hear the range now in view after each change.</li>
    </ul>
</Prose>
